# A piston and crankshaft — the slider-crank, and the last mechanism
# this chapter was missing a joint for.
#
#   mount --HINGE-- crank        the crankshaft in its main bearing
#   crank --BALL-- rod           the big end, on the crank pin
#   rod --BALL-- piston          the little end, on the wrist pin
#   guide --PRISMATIC-- piston   the bore
#
#   5 + 3 + 3 + 5 = 16 rows on 18 freedoms.
#
# WHY BALL JOINTS AND NOT FOUR REVOLUTES. Every pin in a real engine is
# a hinge, and building it that way is the obvious thing to try:
#
#     HINGE 5 + HINGE 5 + HINGE 5 + PRISMATIC 5 = 20 rows on 18
#
# which is over-constrained by two, and IDA will not integrate it (see
# grammar §12.10 — this is the same arithmetic that sinks a two-bearing
# Cardan joint). A planar linkage built out of spatial revolutes is
# redundant: the hinges all insist on the same plane, over and over.
# The standard remedy is the one real multibody models use — give the
# connecting rod SPHERICAL ends. 16 rows leaves two freedoms: the crank
# angle, which is the mechanism, and the rod's spin about its own axis,
# which is passive. Nothing torques the rod about its own length, so
# that freedom simply sits where it started.
#
# THE GEOMETRY IS NOT ARBITRARY. Every joint pivots at the MIDPOINT of
# its pair, so the pins land where they do only because the parts are
# sized to put them there. Writing a for the crank throw and L for the
# rod, the midpoint rule forces the rod's centre to 2a while a rod from
# the crank pin to the wrist pin has its centre at a + L/2. Those agree
# only when
#
#     L = 2a
#
# so this crank throws 0.5 and this rod is 1.0, and every pivot lands on
# a real pin: the main bearing at the origin, the crank pin at 0.5, the
# wrist pin at 1.5. Choose any other ratio and the drawing and the
# mechanism part company.
#
# THE CLOSED FORM. Measuring the wrist pin from the crankshaft axis,
#
#     x(theta) = a cos(theta) + sqrt(L^2 - a^2 sin^2(theta))
#
# exactly — no small-angle anything. The stroke runs from L - a = 0.5 at
# bottom dead centre to L + a = 1.5 at top, and the recording sweeps
# 0.500000 to 1.500000. Against the formula, frame by frame over 300
# frames:
#
#     worst |x - x(theta)| = 8.4e-8      over 1.59 revolutions
#
# and the rod's passive freedom really is passive: its spin about its
# own length measures 0.000 degrees over the whole run.
#
# THE CRANK DOES NOT TURN AT A CONSTANT RATE, and it should not: nothing
# drives it. It was given 2 rad/s at top dead centre and left alone, and
# the rod and piston swap inertia with it through the cycle, so the
# angle wanders ahead of and behind a uniform 2t. The closed form does
# not care — it is a statement about the LINKAGE, relating the piston to
# whatever angle the crank happens to be at, and that is what makes it
# worth checking against a free-running mechanism rather than a driven
# one.
set system.g_constant = 0
set system.uniform_gravity = [0, 0, 0]
collide off
new point as mount { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], inverse_mass = 0 }
new cylinder as crank { mass = 2, radius = 0.55, half_height = 0.05, position = [0, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0, 0, 2] }
# Started at top dead centre, where the piston is momentarily at rest
# and the rod turns about the wrist pin. These are the velocities that
# rotation implies, so g_dot = J u = 0 to roundoff and nothing is
# projected: w_rod = -a w / L about z, and its centre riding at a w / 2.
new cuboid as rod { mass = 0.4, half_extents = [0.5, 0.05, 0.05], position = [1.0, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0, 0, -1], velocity = [0, 0.5, 0] }
new cuboid as piston { mass = 1, half_extents = [0.5, 0.3, 0.3], position = [2.0, 0, 0] }
new point as guide { mass = 1, position = [2.0, 0, 0], inverse_mass = 0 }
hinge mount crank [0, 0, 1]
ball crank rod
ball rod piston
prismatic guide piston [1, 0, 0]
method ida
